<title>BRAZIL: Brazil may hike amount of tax-exempt export sugar.</title>
<headline>Brazil may hike amount of tax-exempt export sugar.</headline>
<dateline>BRASILIA 1996-10-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's Ministry of Industry and Commerce may complete studies on Monday for raising the limit of sugar exports exempt from tax, a senior commodities official said.</p>
<p>Pedro Cabral, head of the sugar and alcohol department, said only the last technical details remained to be solved and a decision could be made on Monday to make tax-free the 20 percent of the sugar export quota that has not yet been exempted.</p>
<p>The government set exports at 5.2 million tonnes of sugar in its 1996/97 crop plan but only exempted an initial 4.2 million tonnes from tax.</p>
<p>Liberating the remaining 1.0 million tonnes was left to a later date to give the ministry an opportunity to monitor the harvest and in particular the supply of alcohol.</p>
<p>-- Michael Christie, Brasilia newsroom 55-61-2230358</p>
</text>
